  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The new labour code of Viet Nam, which came into effect in 2021, requires companies to make significant adjustments of labour practices. When the Vietnam Chamber of Commerce and Industry (VCCI) conducted a survey of 900 enterprises to assess the impact of COVID-19 on labour and employment in August 2020, many employers expressed concern about the unstable industrial relations due to the new labour code. 
VCCI adopted a comprehensive approach to offering member companies with improved legal advisory services to raise awareness and enhance compliance with the labour code, which has been revised based on Viet Nam&apos;s ratification of the ILO Right to Organise and Collective Bargaining Convention, 1949 (No. 98) and subsequent changes to the Labour Code. 
Specifically, VCCI launched a guidebook on industrial relations and human resource management (IR/HR) under the new labour code in March 2021. The guide addresses important topics such as multiple trade unions at the enterprise level, multi-employer collective bargaining, labour contracts and settlement of disputes. VCCI received positive feedback from businesses, especially SMEs, for the guide&apos;s up-to-date nature and user-friendliness. In March-April 2021, VCCI trained 80 trainers from provincial chapters and business associations and delivered training for more than 300 enterprises. They are now providing legal advisory services to member companies. 
To sustain and develop the improved labour law consultation and training services, VCCI created alliances with regional/sectoral associations through an improved network with provincial employer and business associations to respond to the changing business environment under the revised labour law. In addition, one additional legal expert was recruited as Deputy Director of the Bureau for Employer&apos;s Activities (BEA) in VCCI. The VCCI IR/HR Guidebook is being constantly updated by BEA/VCCI with member&apos;s comments, questions, experiences and practices. The ILO supported the BEA/VCCI to conduct a survey and most up-to-date study on the impact of COVID-19 on labour and employment in Viet Nam. The survey aimed to achieve an in-depth understanding of the opportunities and challenges of vaccination, propose recommendations to keep business competitive and sustainable, and propose solutions to mitigate the negative impact of the pandemic on labour and employment. 

The ILO supported VCCI in developing a guidebook on industrial relations and human resource management under the new labour code. In March 2021, the ILO conducted one training workshop in Hanoi and one in Ho Chi Min City for VCCI&apos;s provincial employers&apos; organizations and business associations as well as employers in general on IR/HR Guidebook. The ILO provided advice to address employer concerns and questions in several areas, including labour contracts, labour representative organizations, negotiating with multiple labour organizations within the enterprise, multi-employer collective bargaining and settlement of disputes. 

With the support of the Japan Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare, the ILO conducted a study on how to improve the network between VCCI and its provincial employers&apos; and business associations. In accordance with the recommendations of the report, VCCI is inviting them to important meetings and trainings.  

The ILO supported the training workshop of VCCI-Ho Chi Minh City in March 2021 to promote the application of ILO Fundamental Conventions through enhancing business community capacity in labour dispute resolution system and employment relations for improved compliance towards decent work.</narrative>
   </description>
